    "description": "Historic building restoration combines investigation, material conservation and carefully justified intervention. Iranian examples such as Bam show why structural safety, surviving evidence, traditional construction skills and the surrounding landscape must be considered together. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Restoration", "Historic buildings", "Earthen architecture", "Bam", "Authenticity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:09:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:09:03Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Historic Building Restoration in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore evidence, authenticity and structural care in Iranian building restoration, with lessons from Bam and adaptive reuse in Yazd."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "restoration-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Preserving a building’s evidence",
        "content": "A restored building should remain a credible historical source. The Venice Charter, an influential international conservation text, treats original materials and documentary evidence as foundations for restoration. It rejects conjectural completion and requires respect for significant contributions from different periods. An apparently unified style is therefore not a sufficient reason to remove later historic additions. These are professional conservation principles, rather than a substitute for checking the law applicable to a particular Iranian property. [s1]\n\nThe charter also distinguishes continued care from exceptional restoration work. Maintenance is an ongoing obligation; a socially useful function can help sustain a building, provided adaptation respects its significant layout and decoration. Where parts must be replaced, the intervention should fit the whole while remaining identifiable, so that new work does not falsify the surviving evidence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "structure-and-materials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why diagnosis comes first",
        "content": "The International Council on Monuments and Sites, or ICOMOS, calls for a multidisciplinary approach to structural conservation. Architectural value includes a building’s internal construction, not only its visible appearance. Retaining a facade while removing the historic structure behind it does not meet the organization’s stated conservation criteria. A proposed new use must also be assessed against both conservation requirements and safety conditions. [s2]\n\nThis distinction matters when judging a completed project. A clean exterior or convincing decorative finish cannot, by itself, establish that the building’s structural history has survived. The appropriate questions concern what was investigated, which components were retained and how the intervention serves the building as a whole. This is an editorial application of the ICOMOS principles, not an assessment of any unnamed restoration project. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bam-and-reconstruction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bam: rebuilding after destructive loss",
        "content": "Bam’s citadel in southeastern Iran illustrates the difficult boundary between repair and reconstruction. Its earthen architecture uses layered earth, known as chineh, and sun-dried bricks, or khesht. UNESCO records that the 2003 earthquake collapsed parts of the Governor’s Quarters and upper defensive walls, while also exposing older fabric beneath later restorations. Surviving material and construction knowledge consequently remain essential evidence. [s3]\n\nUNESCO’s conservation requirements call for restoration and selective reconstruction to be grounded in critically assessed documentary and field evidence. They also require attention to the archaeological and natural setting. Recreating a recognizable silhouette is therefore only one consideration: interventions must preserve the relationships that give the larger property its significance. The source describes conservation obligations; it does not establish a current percentage of work completed. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "continued-use",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A future use with a legible past",
        "content": "Yazd provides a complementary example. UNESCO’s property account describes historic structures rehabilitated for university, hotel and restaurant uses while retaining major physical elements and limiting intervention. Such examples show how an existing building can accommodate activity without requiring wholesale replacement. They do not establish that every conversion in Yazd achieves the same standard. [s4]\n\nThe useful conclusion is practical: evaluate restoration through retained evidence, justified changes and continuing care. A building’s future depends on more than the moment a restoration project is declared finished. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
