    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Historic neighborhood conservation addresses the relationships among buildings, streets, infrastructure, livelihoods and community practices. Yazd and Tabriz demonstrate how residential patterns and working commercial networks contribute to the significance of Iranian urban heritage. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Urban heritage", "Neighborhoods", "Yazd", "Tabriz", "Community participation", "Conservation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:09:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:09:03Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Historic Neighborhood Conservation in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand how streets, homes, water systems and working bazaars shape neighborhood conservation in Yazd, Tabriz and other Iranian historic settings."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "neighborhood-as-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The heritage between monuments",
        "content": "A historic neighborhood is more than a collection of individually important buildings. UNESCO’s Historic Urban Landscape recommendation defines urban heritage through accumulated cultural and natural relationships, extending beyond a designated historic centre. Its scope includes infrastructure, water, open space, land use, views and social practices. This approach helps explain why repairing a landmark alone cannot address every change affecting its surroundings. [s1]\n\nThe recommendation aims to connect conservation with social and economic development while sustaining diversity and the quality of the human environment. It recognizes that cities change. The central question is how to manage that change without dismantling the relationships that make a place meaningful. These are international policy principles, not evidence that every Iranian city has implemented them. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "yazd-residential-fabric",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Yazd: streets, shade and shared resources",
        "content": "In Yazd, UNESCO identifies surviving alley patterns, courtyard houses, water cisterns and sabats—partly or fully covered alleys that provide shade—as elements of the historic city. Qanats, underground channels carrying groundwater, connect settlement to the management of scarce water. The neighborhood’s significance emerges from these relationships, rather than from the appearance of earthen walls alone. [s2]\n\nIts social organization also matters. UNESCO discusses waqf, charitable endowments supporting shared facilities, and calls for closer study of relationships between district life and physical structures. Its management requirements include guidance for private owners and earthquake risk research. These are identified conservation needs, not confirmation that all recommended work has been completed. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tabriz-working-networks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tabriz: conserving a functioning urban network",
        "content": "Tabriz Historic Bazaar Complex offers a different model. Covered brick structures and connected spaces accommodate trade, social gatherings, education and religious activity. UNESCO’s assessment emphasizes the close connection between the architectural fabric and the bazaar’s professional and social organization. Protecting this ensemble therefore concerns circulation and relationships among activities as well as individual rooms or roofs. [s3]\n\nThe property account describes a management framework linking conservation objectives with urban planning instruments, technical bodies and operational programmes. This is significant because decisions outside one owner’s premises can affect the larger ensemble. A bazaar cannot be understood adequately through isolated restoration projects when its heritage value depends on interconnected spaces and uses. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to judge proposed change",
        "content": "Read together, these examples suggest useful questions for evaluating a neighborhood project: what shared spaces and connections will remain legible, which activities depend on them, and whose knowledge informs the decision? These questions are an editorial synthesis of the sources, rather than a prescribed Iranian approval procedure. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nYazd directs attention to residential fabric and environmental adaptation; Tabriz highlights commercial interdependence. Neither should become a universal template for every Iranian city. Their common lesson is that conservation works at the scale of relationships. A restored building contributes most clearly to neighborhood heritage when the wider setting and the people using it also remain part of the assessment. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
